From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000006 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sunxi: cubie-a7s: PCIe control pins are on bank PD, not PE

The SoC dtsi names the PCIe control GPIOs as <&pio 4 ...> -- bank PE, pins
PE11 (pwr), PE13 (rst) and PE12 (wake) -- and the controller driver requests
exactly those three by name (pwr-gpios, rst-gpios, wake-gpios). On this board
they are the wrong pins. The schematic routes the nets on bank PD:

  PD20  PCIE_PWR_EN    (schematic p. 7 and p. 14)
  PD21  PCIE-WAKEn     (p. 7 and p. 14)
  PD22  PCIE-PERSTn    (p. 7 and p. 14)
  PD23  PCIE-CLKREQn   (p. 7 and p. 14)

PE11-14 do offer those functions in their mux tables, which is presumably
where the dtsi values come from, but on this PCB they carry other nets
(PE14 is a DP-AUX net). The bank D pins are the wired ones.

PD20 is the one that is easy to miss. The board has no 3.3 V slot rail and
no load switch: the FPC connector feeds an adapter board that carries its own
regulator, and PD20 is that regulator's enable. With PD20 untouched the
device is unpowered, so the PHY sees no receiver and the link never leaves
Detect -- which is what the driver reports as "Link up timeout", followed by
a speed-change timeout against a link that was never up.

Measured on the board, at the U-Boot prompt, before and after:

  as shipped          PORT_DEBUG0 LTSSM 0x00 (Detect), Link Status 0x1011
                      (Gen1, Data Link Layer inactive), `pci` lists the root
                      complex alone, `nvme scan` finds nothing

  with these pins     "pcie link up success", "PCIe speed of Gen3",
                      `pci` lists 01.00.00 0x1c5c:0x1327 (mass storage), and
                      `nvme device` reports the drive: 500118192 x 512

The three properties are all that changes; the driver's own sequence (power
cycle, LTSSM off, PERST low, 100 ms, PERST high, establish link, speed
change) is correct as written and needs no edit. The link comes up on the
first enumeration pass, so no rescan and no retry loop is required either.

Proven by rewriting the three properties in the live control FDT at the
U-Boot prompt (`fdt set`) and running `pci enum` against them, which exercises
the same probe path this patch feeds from flash, without writing to the card.

CLKREQn (PD23) is deliberately not wired up here: the driver does not use it,
and L1 substates are out of scope for the boot loader. It belongs to the
kernel side of this bring-up.

The properties go into the board file rather than the SoC dtsi because they
are board facts. Other A733 boards may well route these nets on PE, and this
overlay has no way to know.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/src/arch/arm/dts/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
+++ b/src/arch/arm/dts/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
@@ -272,6 +272,21 @@
 	status = "okay";
 	pcie1v8-supply = <&reg_bldo1>;
 	pcie3v3-supply = <&reg_dcdc1>;
+
+	/*
+	 * This board routes the PCIe control nets on bank PD, not on the
+	 * bank PE pins the SoC dtsi names: PD20 carries PCIE_PWR_EN, PD22
+	 * PCIE-PERSTn and PD21 PCIE-WAKEn (schematic p7, p14). PE11-14 only
+	 * offer those functions in their mux tables; they are not wired.
+	 *
+	 * PD20 is not optional: there is no slot rail and no load switch on
+	 * the PCB, so that pin gates the regulator on the adapter board the
+	 * FPC connector feeds. Left alone, the device never powers up and
+	 * the link stays in Detect.
+	 */
+	pwr-gpios = <&pio 3 20 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+	rst-gpios = <&pio 3 22 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+	wake-gpios = <&pio 3 21 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 };
 
